00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.010 align:start position:0%
Australian Prime Minister Scott Morrison  seen for himself  the latest areas devastated by bush  fires in New South Wales the day after  he was confronted by angry residents in  the town of Kabongo this woman refusing  to shake his hands the prime minister  said he wanted to offer comfort to those  affected by fires on New Year's Eve and  he's not surprised at their reaction  people are angry it is something that I  will empathize with it is something that  I understand it's not something for me  to take personally it's peak holiday  season but with the state of emergency  declared in the states of New South  Wales and Victoria tens of thousands of  people have been leaving tourist areas  traffic's been backed up for many  kilometres on major highways as people  try to get home or at least to safer  areas about a quarter of the 4,000  people stranded on a beach in the town  of mallacoota in Victoria have been  taken on board naval vessels to escape  the fires haven't been a processor know  well down at the foreshore and it's  something that I want to forget the  evacuations will continue as fire  conditions on Saturday a forecast to be  catastrophic we're making every  preparation possible for what will be a  very difficult day tomorrow weather  conditions are very unpredictable we  know the temperatures will still be in  the mid 40s in many parts of our state  thousands of firefighters are in the  field with additional helicopters on  standby we're unfortunately very likely  we will lose homes tomorrow but we'll be  very happy and we'll call it success if  there's no lives lost that is our single  focus tomorrow a focus on saving lives  in the face of an unprecedented bushfire  season  Felicity Davie al Jazeera Sydney
 

